==English==

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|grc|ἀταξία||disorder}}, derived from {{m|grc|ἄτακτος||disorderly}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈtæksɪə/|/eɪˈtæksɪə/}}
* {{hyph|en|atax|ia}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|pathology}} Lack of [[coordination]] while performing [[voluntary]] [[movements]], which may appear to be [[clumsiness]], [[inaccuracy]], or [[instability]].
#: {{syn|en|ataxy|dystaxia|q2=rare}}
# {{lb|en|chemistry}} The condition of a [[polymer]] in which the [[orientation]] of the [[subunit]]s is [[random]]
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Disorder; irregularity.
#: {{syn|en|dystaxia|q1=obsolete}}
#* '''1569''', {{w|John Lesley|John Leslie}}, ''A Defence of the Honour of the Right Highe, Mightye and Noble Princesse Marie Quene of Scotlande,'' Rheims, Book{{nbsp}}3,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A05352.0001.001]</sup>
#*: Ye frame an other argumente of inconueniences, as thowghe vnder the womans regimente, '''Ataxia''', that ys to saye disorder moste commonlye creapethe in.
#* '''1614''', {{w|Thomas Adams (priest)|Thomas Adams}}, ''The Devills Banket,'' London: Ralph Mab, The Second Service, Sermon{{nbsp}}2, p.{{nbsp}}51,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A00888.0001.001]</sup>
#*: Let not Gods ''eutaxie,'' Order, by our friuolous scruples be brought to '''''ataxie''''', Confusion.
#* '''1640''', {{w|Joseph Hall (bishop)|Joseph Hall}}, ''Episcopacie by Divine Right,'' London: Nathanael Butter, Part{{nbsp}}3, §{{nbsp}}1, p.{{nbsp}}212,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/B13858.0001.001]</sup>
#*: Neither is there any '''''Ataxie''''' to bee feared in bringing in this distinction, betwixt Pastors and flock; It is an ''Eutaxie'' rather:

